# ─── Zorpha indexer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Long-running service. Deploy on Railway with root directory
# `sidequest-protocol/indexer`.

# Chain. RH_TESTNET_RPC_URL is still accepted as an alias for RPC_URL.
#
# THIS FILE IS TESTNET (46630). For mainnet (4663) use .env.mainnet.example --
# every value in this block differs, not just CHAIN_ID.
#
# CHAIN_ID is REQUIRED and has no default. It used to default to 46630, and a
# default is how a mainnet deployment gets labelled testnet: the operator sets
# RPC_URL, the service comes up, and every row it writes carries the wrong
# chain -- which, once written, cannot be told from real testnet history.
RPC_URL=https://rpc.testnet.chain.robinhood.com/rpc
# Comma-separated. viem fails over in order when the primary is unhealthy.
# Both of these answer 46630. Never mix endpoints from different chains here:
# failover is on transport errors, not on chain identity.
RPC_URL_FALLBACK=https://robinhood-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com
CHAIN_ID=46630
EXPLORER_URL=https://explorer.testnet.chain.robinhood.com

# Supabase. The SERVICE ROLE key bypasses RLS so the indexer can write.
# It must never reach the browser — it belongs only in Railway's variables.
SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Contracts. Vaults are normally discovered from the `vaults` table, which the
# deploy script populates; VAULT_ADDRESSES is a manual override.
VAULT_ADDRESSES=
REPUTATION_REGISTRY_ADDRESS=

# Set START_BLOCK to the deployment block ON THIS CHAIN. Leaving it at 0 makes
# the first run scan the whole chain from genesis.
#
# It is read ONLY when a source has no cursor yet. Cursors are chain-scoped
# since migration 012, so each chain gets its own first-run START_BLOCK rather
# than resuming from the other one's height.
#
# Testnet heights are ~113M, mainnet ~55M. A height from the wrong chain is
# rejected at startup by assertChainPreflight() rather than scanning nothing
# forever.
START_BLOCK=0

# Tuning. BLOCK_CHUNK_SIZE must stay under the RPC provider's getLogs range cap
# (commonly 10000). CONFIRMATIONS is how far behind head to stay so a shallow
# reorg is re-scanned rather than written and then contradicted.
# CONFIRMATIONS=3 is ~0.5s of protection at this chain's 0.17s block time,
# which is not protection. The code default is 120 (~20s). Raise it here too
# before pointing anything at a chain where receipts are public.
BLOCK_CHUNK_SIZE=2000
CONFIRMATIONS=3
POLL_INTERVAL_MS=12000
MAX_CONSECUTIVE_FAILURES=10

# Railway injects PORT. Health: /healthz (liveness), /readyz (liveness + DB).
PORT=8080

# ─── Dry run ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DRY_RUN=1 reads the chain, decodes receipts and writes nothing. Supabase
# credentials are NOT required, so the indexing path can be exercised without
# handling a service-role key -- which is why it existed unverified for so long:
# running it at all meant holding the one secret that belongs only in Railway.
#
# It runs a single cycle and exits. Vaults come from VAULT_ADDRESSES and each
# one's type is resolved on chain, since there is no `vaults` table to read.
#
#   DRY_RUN=1 RPC_URL=https://rpc.testnet.chain.robinhood.com/rpc #   START_BLOCK=<recent block> VAULT_ADDRESSES=0x...,0x... #   npx tsx src/index.ts
#
# Proves: RPC reachable, vaults type correctly, event ABIs still match what the
# contracts emit, getLogs returns the receipts, each decodes to a valid row.
# Does NOT prove: the writes, the cursor store, the unique constraints, or
# manager attribution (which reads the `vaults` table).
DRY_RUN=
